     <article class="story single" data-beat="space">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPACE</span> · NASA · 2026-09-24 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Cloudy Cloak Over the Northwest</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Weather</span> <span>#Northwest</span> <span>#Clouds</span> <span>#Atmosphere</span></div>
       <p>A recent atmospheric phenomenon brought a distinct autumn ambiance to the western regions of Washington and Oregon. The area was blanketed by a low-lying cloud layer, creating a pervasive &quot;cloudy cloak&quot; over the landscape, according to observations from NASA. This widespread cloud cover significantly impacted visibility and the general feel of the environment across these states.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/cloudy-cloak-over-the-northwest.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The cloud formation was notably concentrated in the lower elevations of western Washington and Oregon. In contrast, mountainous areas situated along the coast presented a different picture. These higher geographical features remained distinctly above the pall of clouds, enjoying clearer conditions. This stark division illustrates how local topography can play a crucial role in shaping meteorological patterns, with elevated terrains often escaping the atmospheric conditions prevalent in the surrounding lowlands.</p><p>The event provided a clear example of how specific weather systems can introduce a particular seasonal feel, in this case, an autumnal one, even without other typical markers like falling leaves or temperature drops. Such cloud layers are a common feature in certain climates, influencing everything from light quality to local microclimates. The persistent nature of this particular layer contributed to a consistent atmospheric character throughout the affected areas.</p><p>What to watch: Further observations on how local topography influences regional cloud distribution.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the source material with appropriate tone and detail.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/cloudy-cloak-over-the-northwest/" rel="noopener">NASA</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
