     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-09-24 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Hospitals' Body Rejects Hotel Analogy for Room Charges, Urges Balanced Approach</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Healthcare</span> <span>#Policy</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#AHPI</span></div>
       <p>The Association of Healthcare Providers India (AHPI), a prominent hospitals’ body, has rejected the analogy of hotel-like room charges for healthcare facilities, emphasizing the need for a balanced and evidence-based approach to healthcare affordability and accessibility. This stance comes in response to the Parliamentary Standing Committee report titled “Affordability and Accessibility of Healthcare Facilities in Public and Private Sector.”</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/hospitals-body-rejects-hotel-analogy-for-room-charges-urges-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>AHPI stated that it largely welcomes the overall intent and several recommendations outlined in the parliamentary report. However, its rejection of the hotel analogy for room charges highlights a key point of contention. The body argues that healthcare services, including room charges, involve complex medical provisions and infrastructure that differentiate them fundamentally from hospitality services.</p><p>The association's call for a balanced, evidence-based approach suggests a desire for policy decisions to be grounded in comprehensive data and a nuanced understanding of healthcare economics. This includes considering the operational costs, quality of care, and the investments required to maintain and upgrade medical infrastructure.</p><p>AHPI’s response underscores the ongoing dialogue between healthcare providers and policymakers regarding the future of healthcare pricing and accessibility in both public and private sectors. They aim to ensure that reforms consider the unique challenges and requirements of the healthcare industry.</p><p>What to watch: Further discussions between healthcare bodies and the Parliamentary Committee on policy implementation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Article aligns with the source summary’s points about AHPI’s balanced, evidence‑based stance; no contradictory or invented claims found.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/hospitals/hospitals-body-rejects-hotel-analogy-for-room-charges-urges-balanced-approach-to-affordability/134462894" rel="noopener">ET Healthworld</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
