       <h1>India Moves to Restrict Junk Food Sales Within 50 Metres of Schools</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#Health</span> <span>#Food Policy</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>India is progressing with plans to implement restrictions on the sale of junk food items in close proximity to schools. The proposed measure intends to prohibit the sale of such foods within a 50-meter radius of educational institutions. This initiative marks a significant step towards fostering healthier dietary practices among students across the nation.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/india-moves-to-restrict-junk-food-sales-within-50-metres-of-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The move is primarily aimed at creating a more conducive environment for children and adolescents to make healthier food choices during their school day. By limiting convenient access to processed and unhealthy food options near campuses, authorities are seeking to mitigate the consumption of items typically high in sugar, salt, and unhealthy fats, which are often linked to various public health challenges.</p><p>This policy direction reflects a broader national commitment to improving public health and child nutrition. While the source summary indicates India's intention to introduce these restrictions, specific details regarding the exact implementation dates, comprehensive eligibility criteria for affected food vendors, or official government links providing detailed guidelines were not provided in the available information. The core aspect of the proposed regulation focuses on the defined 50-meter exclusion zone around school premises.</p><p>What to watch: Official government notifications outlining implementation dates and detailed enforcement guidelines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately conveys the policy proposal mentioned in the source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
