     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-09-24 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Postpones Cambodia Tiger Reintroduction to 2028</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Wildlife Conservation</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Cambodia</span> <span>#Bengal Tigers</span></div>
       <p>India and Cambodia have jointly decided to postpone their ambitious plan to reintroduce Bengal tigers into Cambodia. The target year for this significant conservation effort has been shifted from 2025 to 2028. This decision follows a comprehensive review that identified a critical challenge: the available prey base within the proposed release landscape is currently insufficient to sustain a viable tiger population.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/india-postpones-cambodia-tiger-reintroduction-to-2028.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The reintroduction initiative aims to bolster tiger numbers in Cambodia, where the big cats are critically endangered or locally extinct in certain areas. Ensuring a robust prey base, consisting of deer, wild boar, and other ungulates, is paramount for the long-term survival and successful breeding of a reintroduced tiger population. Without adequate food sources, the tigers would face severe challenges in establishing themselves and thriving in their new habitat.</p><p>An international consultant to Cambodia’s Ministry of Environment (MoE) for tiger reintroduction was involved in the review process, which underscored the necessity of this postponement. The delay allows for further efforts to enhance the ecosystem's capacity to support the apex predators. This measured approach prioritizes the ecological readiness of the reintroduction sites, aiming to maximize the chances of success for the Bengal tigers. The focus will now shift to habitat restoration and prey population recovery in the intervening years.</p><p>What to watch: Efforts to increase prey populations in Cambodian tiger habitats ahead of the new 2028 target.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the source information regarding the postponement of the tiger reintroduction project.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/short-article/2026/09/india-postpones-cambodia-tiger-reintroduction/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
