     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-09-24 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India's Tribal Jewellery: A Tapestry of Nature, Trade, and Identity</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India</span> <span>#Tribal</span> <span>#Jewellery</span> <span>#Culture</span></div>
       <p>Across India's tribal communities, traditional jewellery weaves fascinating narratives of nature, trade, and identity. Ornaments often feature unexpected combinations of materials like shells, tusks, beads, bone, feathers, and metals, transforming into necklaces, anklets, and arm ornaments. Among the Sumi Nagas, the Aminihu necklace combines boar tusks with cane, a conch-shell button, and a carnelian bead, historically linked to warrior status. Similarly, Angami Tsubo uses carnelian, glass beads, and ivory or bone.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/indias-tribal-jewellery-a-tapestry-of-nature-trade-and-ident.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A compelling aspect is the discovery of seashells in Tangkhul Naga ornaments like the Kongsang and Huishon, despite their inland location. This highlights extensive historical trade networks, where materials travelled far before becoming deeply local expressions of culture. In Odisha’s Niyamgiri hills, Dongria Kondh women wear brass anklets and neckbands, which were historically exchanged with Ghasi metalworkers for agricultural produce like ragi, maize, turmeric, and vegetables, underscoring a symbiotic community relationship. These examples reveal how natural elements are imbued with profound meaning, carrying stories of origin, exchange, and personal and collective history within each piece.</p><p>Why it matters: These ornaments are living archives, preserving ancient trade routes, ecological knowledge, and the intricate social fabric of communities.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Faithful to source summary; no contradictions.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/conservation/nature/india-tribal-jewellery-boar-tusks-cowrie-shells-dongria-kondh-naga-traditions-12571937" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
