     <article class="story single" data-beat="space">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPACE</span> · NASA · 2026-09-24 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NASA's SpaceX Crew-13 Mission to Intensify Human Health and Performance Studies on ISS</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#NASA</span> <span>#SpaceX Crew-13</span> <span>#ISS</span> <span>#human health</span></div>
       <p>NASA's SpaceX Crew-13 mission to the International Space Station (ISS) will focus extensively on understanding human health and performance in microgravity. During their tenure aboard the orbiting laboratory, astronauts will participate in a comprehensive series of biomedical and human performance investigations designed to advance knowledge critical for future space exploration.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/nasas-spacex-crew-13-mission-to-intensify-human-health-and-p.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A key element of these studies includes a new collaborative effort specifically aimed at examining how spaceflight impacts blood flow and clotting mechanisms in the human body. This research is vital for addressing potential health risks associated with prolonged exposure to the space environment.</p><p>Beyond physiological responses, NASA will also assess crew members' manual piloting skills, an essential aspect of operational capabilities for both current and future missions. Furthermore, the Crew-13 mission will involve evaluating various methods to counter vision and brain changes that can occur during extended periods in space. The astronauts will also be engaged in gathering essential health data, contributing to a broader understanding of human adaptation and resilience in extraterrestrial conditions. These investigations underscore NASA's commitment to ensuring astronaut well-being and mission success.</p><p>What to watch: Upcoming reports on the preliminary findings from these critical health studies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Aligned with source; no unsupported statements.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/nasa-to-study-human-health-performance-during-crew-13-mission/" rel="noopener">NASA</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-09-24/nasas-spacex-crew-13-mission-to-intensify-human-health-and-p" data-title="NASA's SpaceX Crew-13 Mission to Intensify Human Health and Performance Studies on ISS"><b>Share:</b>
         <a class="sh-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M8 1.8A6.2 6.2 0 0 0 2.7 11L1.8 14.2l3.3-.9A6.2 6.2 0 1 0 8 1.8z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>WhatsApp</a> ·
         <a class="sh-tg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M14.4 1.6 1.6 7l3.6 1.2L6 13l2.2-3.1 3.4 2.4 2.8-10.7z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Telegram</a> ·
