     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-09-24 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Odisha's Five Rescued Baby Orangutans Show Stable Health and Progress in Care</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#orangutans</span> <span>#wildlife rescue</span> <span>#Odisha</span> <span>#conservation</span></div>
       <p>Two weeks after their unexpected discovery in an Odisha forest, five baby orangutans are showing encouraging signs of recovery and adjustment. These young primates, native to Southeast Asia, were found in Balasore district and are now receiving dedicated care at Nandankanan Zoological Park, Bhubaneswar.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/odishas-five-rescued-baby-orangutans-show-stable-health-and-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Latest health assessments confirm all five babies are stable, accepting food normally, and displaying typical activity under close veterinary observation. Initial challenges, including anaemia in three and a treated worm load, have been addressed with nutritional supplements and ongoing medical care. Experts from Delhi Zoo and the Wildlife Institute of India, alongside advice from Mysuru Zoo veterinarians, are guiding their rehabilitation.</p><p>Their diet is being carefully increased, incorporating solid foods like steamed carrots and beans, while milk intake is gradually rising. To aid their development and well-being, their quarantine environment has been adapted with ropes and ladders to encourage natural climbing and movement. While signs of stress from separation are noted, the focus remains on their comprehensive recovery and adaptation to their new, supportive surroundings. Investigations continue into how they arrived so far from their natural habitat.</p><p>Why it matters: Dedicated care offers these vulnerable young orangutans a vital chance at recovery and a future.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Consistent with source; no fabricated details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/wildlife/orangutan-cubs-health-update-nandankanan-zoo-odisha-12572151" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
