       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · News18 India · 2026-09-24 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Rahul Gandhi Cites EC Dissent Notes Amidst Electoral Roll Concerns</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Politics</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Election Commission</span> <span>#Rahul Gandhi</span></div>
       <p>Congress leader Rahul Gandhi has drawn attention to dissent notes submitted by two Election Commissioners, Sukhbir Singh Sandhu and Vivek Joshi. These notes reportedly pertain to the Special Intensive Revision (SIR) of electoral rolls, a process crucial for updating voter lists across the country.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/rahul-gandhi-cites-ec-dissent-notes-amidst-electoral-roll-co.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Mr. Gandhi’s remarks highlight a perceived internal disagreement within the Election Commission of India regarding the handling or outcomes of the SIR process. The specific details of the dissent notes, as referenced by Mr. Gandhi, indicate concerns raised by the commissioners regarding certain aspects of the electoral roll revision.</p><p>The Special Intensive Revision is a periodic exercise undertaken by the Election Commission to ensure the accuracy and completeness of electoral rolls, allowing citizens to register, update, or correct their voter information. Any dissent related to this process could signify differing opinions on its methodology, transparency, or potential implications for the fairness of future elections.</p><p>This development brings the internal workings of the Election Commission under scrutiny, particularly concerning major electoral procedures. The comments by Mr. Gandhi underscore the political sensitivity surrounding electoral processes and the importance of perceived impartiality and thoroughness in their execution.</p><p>What to watch: Further developments regarding the Election Commission's response and any official statements on the SIR dissent notes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article correctly reports on the dissent notes mentioned in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/india/elections-being-rigged-rahul-gandhi-targets-ec-amid-sir-dissent-row-says-gyanesh-kumar-should-resign-10350950.html" rel="noopener">News18 India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
