       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-09-24 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>White House Accused of 'Repeatedly Violating' Court Order on Press Access</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#White House</span> <span>#press access</span> <span>#media freedom</span> <span>#court order</span></div>
       <p>Leading media organizations have accused the White House of repeatedly violating a court order related to press access. According to reports from these outlets, the administration has continued to restrict entry for journalists, despite a judicial directive aimed at restoring their access.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-24/white-house-accused-of-repeatedly-violating-court-order-on-p.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Employees representing news organizations such as CNN, Politico, and MS Now have come forward with accounts of being denied entry to White House premises. These incidents reportedly occurred even after the court order was issued, raising concerns among the press corps.</p><p>Further compounding the issue, some journalists reported that their press credentials were confiscated during these attempts to gain access. This action has been highlighted by the media outlets as part of the alleged violations of the court's mandate. The ongoing dispute centers on the parameters of media access to the executive branch.</p><p>The allegations suggest a pattern of non-compliance with the court's decision, as asserted by the affected media entities. The reported denial of entry and confiscation of passes by the White House are central to the claims made by CNN, Politico, and MS Now regarding press freedom and adherence to judicial rulings.</p><p>What to watch: Further developments in the legal challenge and media access policies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Matches source summary; no extra assertions.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
